\section{Basic, Stochastic, and Mini-Batch Gradient Descent}

This assignment will study different ways to optimize common objective functions in many areas of Machine Learning, namely, stochastic gradient descent. Here we will test to implement these optimizers for a well-known model, logistic regression. See \citet{ruder2016overview} for pseudo-code and details on the algorithms.

We are going to work with this data as a test case:

<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
library(uuml)
data("binary")

binary$gre_sd <- (binary$gre - mean(binary$gre))/sd(binary$gre)
binary$gpa_sd <- (binary$gpa - mean(binary$gpa))/sd(binary$gpa)
X <- model.matrix(admit ~ gre_sd + gpa_sd, binary)
y <- binary$admit
@


\subsection{Implement the gradient for logistic regression}


The likelihood function for logistic regression is
\[
L(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}) = \prod^n_{i=1} p_i^{y_i} (1 - p_i)^{1-y_i} \,,
\]
where
\[
\log \frac{p_i}{1-p_i} = \mathbf{x}_i \theta  \,,
\]
and $\mathbf{x}_i$ is the $i$th row of the design matrix $\mathbf{X}$ and $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^P$ is a $P \times 1$ column vector of parameters.

Maximizing the likelihood is equivalent to minimizing the \emph{mean negative log-likelihood}, which we denote by $\text{NLL}$ throughout this assignment:
\begin{align}
\text{NLL}(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X})
 &= -\frac{1}{n}\log L(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}) \notag\\
 &= -\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left[y_i\log(p_i)+(1-y_i)\log(1-p_i)\right] \notag\\
 &= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left[\log(1+\exp(\mathbf{x}_i\theta))-y_i\mathbf{x}_i\theta\right].
\end{align}
This is the binary cross-entropy averaged over observations. Both the loss and its gradient use this mean convention; no additional division by $n$ is needed after computing either one.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (4p) Derive the gradient of the mean negative log-likelihood $\text{NLL}(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X})$ with respect to $\theta$.\\\emph{Hint!} See \citet[Ch. 4.4]{hastie2009elements}.
\item (2p) Implement the gradient as \texttt{nll\_grad(y, X, theta)}. It should return the gradient of the mean loss $\text{NLL}$ defined above. Show that your implementation reproduces the following two test cases to four decimal places in your report.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad <- function(y, X, theta){
c("(Intercept)" = 0.1825, "gre_sd" = -0.0857, "gpa_sd" = -0.0829)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0))
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad <- function(y, X, theta){
c("(Intercept)" = -0.0217, "gre_sd" = 0.0395, "gpa_sd" = 0.0426)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad(y, X, theta = c(-1,0.5,0.5))
@

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Implement Gradient Descent}
Use the following function to evaluate the mean negative log-likelihood. It converts the total log-likelihood supplied by \texttt{uuml} to the mean loss defined above. Use \texttt{nll()} for loss evaluation throughout this assignment.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll <- function(y, X, theta){
  -uuml::ll(y, X, theta) / length(y)
}
theta <- c(0, 0, 0)
nll(y, X, theta)
@

\emph{Hint!} Gradient descent subtracts the gradient of the loss. Since \texttt{nll\_grad()} already returns the gradient of the mean negative log-likelihood, do not negate it or divide it by the number of observations again.

For SGD and mini-batch updates, evaluate \texttt{nll\_grad()} on the current observation or mini-batch, averaging over the observations supplied to that call. To monitor training, evaluate \texttt{nll()} on \emph{all training observations} at the end of each epoch, so every algorithm's loss history uses the same scale.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Run logistic regression in R to get an MLE estimate of \texttt{theta} using the \texttt{glm()} function. Print the three parameter estimates.

\emph{Hint!} The design matrix \texttt{X} already include an intercept so you need to remove that either from \texttt{X} or from the R formula using \texttt{y ~ -1 + X}.
\item Implement the following gradient descent algorithms \citep[see][for details and psuedo-code]{ruder2016overview} as three separate R functions. Print these functions in your report.

\emph{Note!} Each epoch should include all data points. A tip is to order the data points randomly in each iteration.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) ordinary (full/batch) gradient descent
\item (2p) stochastic gradient descent
\item (2p) mini-batch (stochastic) gradient descent using ten samples to estimate the gradient
\end{enumerate}



A boilerplate for the algorithm can be found here.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
mbsgd <- function(y, X, sample_size, eta, epochs){
  # Setup output matrix
  results <- matrix(0.0, ncol = ncol(X) + 2L, nrow = epochs)
  colnames(results) <- c("epoch", "nll", colnames(X))

  # Run algorithm
  theta <- rep(0.0, ncol(X)) # Init theta to the 0 vector
  for(j in 1:epochs){

    ### Put the algorithm code here ###

    # Store epoch, mean NLL and parameter values
    results[j, "epoch"] <- j
    results[j, "nll"] <- nll(y, X, theta)
    results[j, -(1:2)] <- theta
  }
  return(results)
}
@
The function can then be called as:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
result <- mbsgd(y, X, sample_size = 10, eta = 0.1, epochs = 500)
@

\item Try different learning parameters $\eta$ and run the algorithm for roughly 500 epochs. You should find three different $\eta$ for each algorithm. One $\eta$ where the optimizer diverge, one $\eta$ where the optimizer converge very slowly and one $\eta$ that converges quick(er). Note that $\eta$ should be fixed/the same during all 500 epochs. Visualize your results in two plots per algorithm and choice of $\eta$ as a line graph (hence 18 figures in total).
\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) The mean negative log-likelihood \texttt{nll(y, X, theta)} evaluated on \emph{all training observations} for the current $\theta$ (y-axis), against epochs (full data iterations, x-axis).
\item (2p) The value of one $\theta$ parameter of your choice (y-axis) and the epochs (full data iterations, x-axis). Also include the true values you got from using the \texttt{glm()} function above as a horizontal line in the figure.
\end{enumerate}
Below is an illustration of how to plot the mean loss over epochs using \texttt{ggplot2}. These are simulated plotting values, not optimizer output; use your algorithm's recorded results for the required plots.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE, fig.height=2>>=
library(ggplot2)
set.seed(4711)
example_results <- data.frame(
  epoch = 1:500,
  nll = rnorm(500, mean = 1.5, sd = 0.1)
)
ggplot(example_results, aes(x = epoch, y = nll)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1.5, color = "blue") +
  labs(x = "Epoch", y = "Mean negative log-likelihood",
       title = "Illustrative mean-loss history")
@
\end{enumerate}


