\section{* Gradient Descent for penalized logistic regression}

To get a pass with distinction point, you can choose between this task or the task below.

\input{distinction_task.tex}

Here we use the same data as in task 1 above.

<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(uuml)
data("binary")

binary$gre_sd <- (binary$gre - mean(binary$gre))/sd(binary$gre)
binary$gpa_sd <- (binary$gpa - mean(binary$gpa))/sd(binary$gpa)
X <- model.matrix(admit ~ gre_sd + gpa_sd, binary)
y <- binary$admit
@

As in task 1, we minimize the mean negative log-likelihood. With a ridge penalty, the loss is
\begin{align}
\text{NLL}_r(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}, \lambda) = \text{NLL}(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}) + \frac{\lambda}{2} \sum_{j=2}^P \theta_j^2\,,
\end{align}
where $\text{NLL}$ is the mean negative log-likelihood defined in task 1 and $\lambda \geq 0$. The first column of $\mathbf{X}$ is the intercept column, so $\theta_1$ is excluded from the penalty \citep[see][Ch. 3.4]{hastie2009elements}.

\emph{Note!} The loss uses the \emph{mean} negative log-likelihood plus the penalty. Do not divide the loss or its gradient by $n$ a second time. This convention matches the logistic ridge objective in \texttt{glmnet}; changing the scaling of the likelihood changes the meaning of $\lambda$. More information can be found \href{https://glmnet.stanford.edu/articles/glmnet.html#logistic-regression-family-binomial-}{here}.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) Derive the the gradient for $\text{NLL}_r(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X},\lambda)$ with respect to $\theta$.
\item (1p) Implement the gradient as \texttt{nllr\_grad(y, X, theta, lambda)}. It should return the gradient of $\text{NLL}_r$, including the penalty, with the intercept unpenalized. Show that your implementation reproduces the following three test cases to four decimal places in your report.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
c("(Intercept)" = 0.1825, "gre_sd" = -0.0857, "gpa_sd" = -0.0829)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0), lambda = 0)
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
c("(Intercept)" = 0.1825, "gre_sd" = -0.0857, "gpa_sd" = -0.0829)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0), lambda = 1)
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
    c("(Intercept)" = -0.0217, "gre_sd" = 0.5395, "gpa_sd" = 0.5426)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad(y, X, theta = c(-1,0.5,0.5), lambda = 1)
@
\item (1p) Implement the penalized loss $\text{NLL}_r$ as \texttt{nllr(y, X, theta, lambda)}. Show that your implementation reproduces the following three test cases to four decimal places in your report.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
0.6931
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0), lambda = 0)
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
0.6931
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0), lambda = 1)
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
0.8613
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr(y, X, theta = c(-1,0.5,0.5), lambda = 1)
@
\item (1p) Use \texttt{glmnet} to estimate \texttt{theta} for logistic ridge regression. Set \texttt{family = "binomial"}, \texttt{lambda = 1}, \texttt{alpha = 0}, and \texttt{standardize = FALSE} so the fit uses the same loss as your implementation on the already standardized predictors. Remove the intercept column from \texttt{X} when calling \texttt{glmnet}, and retain its default \texttt{intercept = TRUE} so it estimates an unpenalized intercept.
\item (2p) Implement the following gradient descent algorithms to minimize $\text{NLL}_r$. Use \texttt{nllr\_grad} and subtract the gradient in each update:
\begin{enumerate}
\item ordinary (full or batch) gradient descent
\item mini-batch (stochastic) gradient descent using ten samples to estimate the gradient
\end{enumerate}
\item (2p) Try different learning parameters $\eta$ and $\lambda$. When does the algorithm converge or diverge? Visualize the epochs (x-axis) and the penalized loss $\text{NLL}_r$ evaluated on all observations (y-axis). Show at least one plot per algorithm that converges.
\end{enumerate}

